 gamelan music and music from other parts  of asia  in other words revels music is quite  multicultural emerging from this mixture  of qualities are also key elements of  revels personal voice which is like a  combination of childlike simplicity  flamboyant virtuosity innovative  movement of harmony strict forms and  symmetrical proportions mechanical  precision of rhythmic and melodic ideas  and an imaginative palette of musical  textures in other words revels music  makes me imagine a children's story  perhaps about a group of friends maybe a  nostalgic french person a shy jazz  musician and a spanish dancer all  traveling on an asian wooden boat to  cross the river to enter a Louisville  castle where they come across a carnival  and all get lost but then find a village  of people huddled around a campfire that  serve them warm soup now following the  tradition of these how to sound like  videos where I honor the composer's  birthday let's go directly to the piano  and look at how all of these things  might translate into music Ravel is very  ambiguous that's why I like him  all right so let's focus we have  obviously the melody  [Music]  happy birthday I want to fragment the  melody so that the beginning just really  focuses on these two pairs I think there  needs to be a little more structure and  a little more momentum also I'm choosing  a closer voicing so all these notes are  pretty close together versus wider any  spacing that has these rubs I'm trying  to go for something with a waltz like  quality but this is a little too obvious  when Ravel makes something sweet it has  a little undertone of something  nostalgic no this is a little too suave  [Music]  okay so that wraps up that section  moving on Ravel has a lot of sections  where you have one space and then you go  to another place and you come back then  you go back this sort of back and forth  thing we need something otherworldly  okay I like that  this is a little too mushy maybe it's  time for another flourish so something  kind of like how water spews out  somewhere we need we need more more  happening some more bubbles in the water  [Music]  okay so we have enough bubbles and  enough splashes  [Music]  now we need to return to somewhere where  it's delicate  so even when something is extremely  delicate in Ravel's music there's always  something very solid about it something  that just holds everything together  almost like a very delicate flower but  frozen inside a geometric a perfectly  shaped geometric block of ice so  something like this  is a little too loose  okay  we're gonna  switch that node around a lot of times  you find cords that need to resolve like  a dominant seventh chord but instead of  actually resolving  maybe whole string  several together and just keep going  slightly heartbroken yes okay so so  something like this where I imagine just  more lacing this I'm gonna have to  practice this later but  [Music]  it's almost like seeing something lit in  a way where you're not quite sure  whether it's getting darker or getting  lighter  okay this is a little Debussy  [Music]  maybe just  whoops  okay I'm going to borrow from some  things that I learned from doing that  list video where we're gonna have to use  both right and left hand for the melody  here  [Music]  a little tremolo that's interlocked  either that or maybe both but there's  just one more little leaf that kind of  blows in the air at the end okay I have  enough notes now to practice this and  while I do that over the next few days  I'll probably have a better idea of what  things I want to keep and I'll have some  adjustments made along the way so I'll  check in with you in a few days okay so  on second thought right there I feel  like it's a little too Christmasy a  little yeah a little Christmasy so I'm  going to change that a little bit if I  can maybe it's too late  maybe I'll just I'll just thin it out a  little bit  and play it in a matter-of-fact way so  not give it too much attention or too  much sweetness yet now that we've worked  out the arrangement we must talk about  how it's going to be played because that  is a huge part of it for example just  the opening if I played this with a  different type of articulation that  would be a little more his Spanish voice  so less pedal doesn't really work as  well so just having enough pedal and a  certain kind of uniformity to the sound  but also with a little bit of focus on  the tops maybe part like this outlining  the top and the octave so that the sound  has a little bit of focus in a place  like this it helps to keep your fingers  pretty limp so that they're really  relaxed and it's almost as if they're  hanging from your wrists but I mean  obviously I'm exaggerating here but  keeping it loose allows you to play with  a little more delicate touch regarding  time and pacing I'm going to be using as  little rubato as possible so nothing too  exaggerated in terms of pushing and  pulling the time so much for example  something like this  [Music]  is with a lot of rubato so instead I'm  going to keep it much more regular and  conservative in terms of taking too much  time in places  [Music]  okay I think I'm ready to record  [Music]  you  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  that is all for now thank you so much  for watching and a special thank you to  my patrons on patreon

